Stepper-Ninja Source-Only Change Log

Date: 2026-10-14

StepperNinja Mach3 USB - first development version available for testing
- Provide an installable test package in configurations/StepperNinja-Mach3-USB/
  with a Win32 x86 Mach3 plugin and USB-only UF2 firmware for Pico/RP2040
  and Pico 2/RP2350 (ARM). USB operation does not require WIZnet hardware.
- Introduce a separate mach3-3axis firmware profile with three STEP/DIR axes,
  seven digital inputs, six digital outputs and one spindle PWM output.
  The default LinuxCNC profile retains its existing flexible configuration.

Motion and controller implementation
- Stream Mach3 XYZ trajectories as buffered 1 ms step records through a
  256-record controller queue. Track accepted and executed records separately;
  report emitted STEP counts and the executed program line back to Mach3.
  STEP counts are pulse feedback, not encoder-based mechanical position.
- Generate synchronised axis pulse trains with PIO. Support up to 200 steps
  per millisecond per axis (200 kHz average), subject to STEP high/low time
  and DIR setup checks; reject incompatible Motor Tuning settings.
- Implement local acceleration/deceleration for jog and controller-side moves,
  sequential axis homing with fast search, backoff and slow re-approach,
  configurable homing travel limits/offsets, and controller-side dwell.
- Integrate normal Feed Hold with Mach3's planned deceleration: drain the
  braking trajectory and accept the new trajectory supplied by Cycle Start.
- Implement XYZ G31 probing with contact capture separate from the braking
  endpoint. Publish work-coordinate results to ProbeX/Y/Z and parameters
  #2000..#2002; handle probe cancellation and failure to reach contact.
- Map spindle enable/direction, mist, flood and an auxiliary output, with
  spindle speed ratio driving PWM. Support input/output and DIR inversion.
- Handle E-stop, limits, watchdog expiry, queue underflow and pulse timing
  faults in the controller; propagate faults to Mach3 Reset/E-stop handling.
  Reconnection starts with E-stop asserted and requires a Reset cycle.

Communication and Mach3 integration
- Use motion protocol v3 with session ownership, CRC, ordered commands,
  acknowledgements, retries and duplicate-command suppression. USB CDC uses
  framed binary messages and session-based handover from the serial console.
  The driver also supports UDP with the corresponding network firmware;
  the packaged UF2 files are USB-only. Install matching firmware and DLL.
- Add profile-specific USB/COM or UDP settings, timeouts, watchdog, PWM,
  STEP/DIR timing and homing parameters. Read axis scale, velocity and
  acceleration from Mach3 Motor Tuning.
- Add seven named input-inversion checkboxes and synchronise Mach3 Active Low
  for all signals mapped to each DI, including shared home/limit assignments.
- Add an approximately 50 ms live input monitor showing physical H/L and
  logical ON/off states with immediate inversion preview. The selected
  controller reuses its existing connection; Save applies settings and
  Cancel preserves them. Monitoring leaves the controller disarmed.
- Use the official Plugin Wizard plain-data ABI for Mach3-owned structures,
  with compile-time layout checks to correct Motor Tuning/input access.
  Fix MFC startup state handling, use an XML-safe plugin identifier and
  disable the wrapper's request to start Mach3's parallel-port engine.
- Show the last runtime driver error and Win32 COM errors in diagnostics.
  Include sn-mach3-usb-check.exe for CLAIM/POLL/RELEASE connection checks
  without configuring, arming or moving the controller.

Ready-to-try profiles and validation status
- Include separate XYZ mill and XZ radius-mode lathe profiles, launchers and
  macro folders; manual M6 tool changes do not perform an automatic traverse.
  Base profiles use 100 steps/mm, 10,000 mm/min and 250 mm/s^2; separate
  Mill1000/Turn1000 presets use 1000 steps/mm for higher step-rate testing.
- Include English/Hungarian setup instructions, wiring PDFs, a GPIO table,
  build metadata and SHA256 checksums. Add targeted Git ignore exceptions
  for the packaged DLL and both UF2 files so the test package is complete.
- The package records 10/10 Windows tests passing, x86 DLL startup/loading,
  physical RP2040 USB communication and live input monitoring, and Mach3
  Motor Tuning plus E-stop/Reset integration checks. See the package README
  and package-info.json for the recorded validation scope.
- This is the first version packaged for user trials, not a machine-validated
  release. Physical motion, homing, G31, full lathe operation and the 200 kHz
  waveform still require hardware validation; physical input-switch toggling
  and RP2350 operation are also unverified. Spindle encoder feedback and
  synchronised threading are not implemented; spindle calibration remains
  machine-specific.

Date: 2026-10-09

Encoder index latch protocol v2
- Keep raw PIO counts continuous; latch the IRQ count and calculate position
  using a host offset, following LinuxCNC encoder/HostMot2 semantics.
- Use HAL_IO index-enable, persistent completion and request tags to prevent
  repeated high commands, delayed replies and lost responses from rearming
  or completing the wrong request.
- Synchronize command/snapshot with the core-0 index IRQ; core-1 watchdog
  requests disarming rather than resetting PIO/substep state concurrently.
- Calculate velocity from matching raw-count/time deltas across packet loss.
- Packet format changed: update firmware AND driver together. Reject a
  mismatched protocol marker; no automatic legacy fallback or deployment.
- Add shared firmware state-machine and full-driver regression coverage.
- Physical latch accuracy, live homing and any reported segfault still need
  actual hardware validation. See docs/ENCODER-INDEX.md (Hungarian: .hu.md).

Date: 2026-10-06

LinuxCNC 2.9 / 2.10 HAL API compatibility
- Added hal-driver/hal_compat.h and migrated stepgen-ninja pin types,
  creation, value access and callback registration to the shared adapter.
- Based on Frederic Müller (FredericM88)'s MIT-licensed compatibility code:
  https://github.com/FredericM88/linuxcnc-sim
  Preserved the original license in hal-driver/LICENSE.hal-compat.txt and
  added attribution to LICENSE.txt and all language variants of README.
- Migrated Board 0, 1, 2, 3 and 100 helpers, the custom-board template,
  PWM and Raspberry Pi SPI pin accesses; retained test-branch spidev/libgpiod.
- Fixed checksum-error handling to clear the connected pin value while
  retaining its reference; fixed compilation of encoder-free Board 2.
- Migrated lubrication-guard.c and hal_util.c/.h to the same HAL adapter.
  Retained lubrication pin contracts, inversion, timing and fault/reset logic.
- Added the lubrication-guard CMake module target and included it in
  hal-modules, with separate component installation support.
- Added module and utility contract tests, creation/export/ready failure
  checks, profile compilation and cross-version transcript comparisons.
- Validation on x86-64: LinuxCNC 2.9.10 produced both loadable modules and
  passed 30/30 tests; real 2.10.0-pre2 headers compiled both module objects
  and passed 33/33 tests. Lubrication and utility transcripts also matched
  their unchanged original 2.9 sources.
- Live Raspberry Pi/HAL hardware validation remains pending.

Date: 2026-04-04

Intent
- This log focuses on source-level changes only.
- The top-level project is treated as the new version.

Added Files (high-impact explanation)
- A full WIZnet ioLibrary tree was added under firmware/ioLibrary_Driver/.
	This brings Ethernet and Internet protocol support sources (DHCP, DNS, MQTT, SNTP, TFTP, HTTP, SNMP).
- New/expanded breakout board and flash programming modules were added in firmware/modules/.
- Quadrature encoder substep implementation and support files were added in firmware/quadrature_encoder_substep/.
- HAL-side feature modules and utilities were expanded in hal-driver/ and hal-driver/modules/.
- New top-level configuration header was added: pio_settings.h.
- Separated BREAKOUTBOARD handling files.
- Added 2 new BREAKOUTBOARD variants (2 and 3) with associated support files.
- Added new USER BREAKOUTBOARD variant with associated support files (breakoutboard_user.c).

